WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.960
Harvest-now, decrypt-later risk should be communicated without panic.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:07.780
Leadership does not need speculative countdowns or dramatic claims.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:17.440
Leaders need to understand that some encrypted data may remain valuable long after collection, and that future advances could weaken assumptions behind certain public-key protections.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:28.220
The useful message is practical: identify long-lived sensitive data, understand dependencies, prioritize action, and make migration possible before urgency forces rushed decisions.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.920
Governance turns that message into a program.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:41.860
Risk registers can track systems with long confidentiality lifetimes, exposed data flows, unresolved cryptographic dependencies, vendor blockers, and accepted residual risk.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:52.560
Migration roadmaps can define phases for inventory, pilots, procurement updates, standards alignment, vendor coordination, testing, staged deployment, and monitoring.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:01:01.260
Policy updates can describe approved cryptographic approaches, retention expectations, exception handling, and documentation requirements.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.280
Procurement and vendor management are part of the work.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:19.620
Organizations should ask suppliers about cryptographic dependencies, PQC readiness, roadmap assumptions, supported protocols, certificate strategy, library support, hardware constraints, monitoring capabilities, and customer configuration options.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.360
Vendor answers should be treated as inputs, not guarantees.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:31.420
Teams still need testing, documentation, and contingency plans when roadmap dates shift or support arrives unevenly across products.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.680
Program planning should document assumptions and uncertainty.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:42.340
Nobody can responsibly promise an exact future threat date or claim that a single technology eliminates all cryptographic risk.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:50.960
Good communication explains priorities, owners, blockers, decisions needed, funding needs, dependency risks, and measurable progress.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:59.740
This keeps harvest-now, decrypt-later planning grounded in governance and engineering rather than fear, slogans, or abstract cryptographic theory.
